[interviewer · INTRO] Your client is Brew & Bean, a specialty coffee chain with 200 locations across the US. Over the past two years, their net profit margin has declined from 24% to 6%, even though revenues have grown 15% over the same period. The CEO has hired us to identify the root cause of this profit decline and recommend how to reverse it. How would you approach this problem?

Take a moment, then walk me through how you'd approach this.

[candidate · 0:06] Let me take a second to structure this.

So revenue is up 15% but net margin fell from 24% to 6% — that's a huge drop, roughly three-quarters of their margin gone. Since revenue grew, the problem is almost certainly on the cost side, or costs growing much faster than revenue.

I'd break it into two branches. First, revenue: is the 15% growth coming from new stores, more transactions per store, or higher ticket size? That matters because opening stores adds fixed cost. Second, costs: split into COGS — coffee beans, milk, cups — and operating costs like labor, rent, utilities, plus any corporate overhead or marketing.

One thing I'd want early: do we have menu-price history over the two years? Whether they've taken price helps me read both branches.

Then I'd benchmark each cost line as a percent of revenue, two years ago versus today, and see which one moved most. Can we start with the cost breakdown?
