[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 0:52] A straight-line extrapolation to a loss. I don't have why management held prices. Setting that aside, beyond what we've discussed so far, what else could Brew & Bean do to address this, and which of those ideas would you pursue first?

[candidate · 0:59] Beyond pricing, I'd put levers in three buckets.

On sourcing: lock in forward contracts or hedge green coffee rather than buying spot, consolidate volume across 200 stores for better terms, and look at supplier renegotiation or dual-sourcing origins. Also reformulation — smaller default milk pour, cheaper cup spec — which cuts input per unit without touching the menu board.

On mix: push customers toward higher-margin items. Black coffee and espresso are mostly bean cost; dairy-heavy and food items carry the inflated 75% bucket. Merchandising and bundling can shift mix without a visible price increase.

On overhead: 14% of revenue for a 200-store chain, growing a point a year, suggests corporate isn't scaling. Worth a zero-based review.

I'd pursue pricing first. Prices haven't moved in two years while inputs rose 38%, so there's almost certainly headroom, and it's the only lever fast enough given they're under a year from losses. Sourcing is second — real savings but 6-12 months to land.
