[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 1:01] Pricing first, sourcing second. Anything beyond cost and price levers, say on the revenue side or the customer side, that could help close the gap?

[candidate · 1:08] Yes, a few. On ticket size, attachment is probably the biggest one — food attach rate on a coffee order, or upsizing. Even if food carries inflated input costs, an incremental item still adds absolute gross profit on a visit they're already paying rent and labor for. Since labor is fixed at 22% and stable, anything that drives revenue per store flows through at a better rate.

On frequency, a loyalty or subscription program — prepaid monthly coffee — lifts visits and gives them cash up front and better demand visibility for sourcing.

On dayparts, if they're coffee-heavy in the morning, afternoon and evening capacity is sitting idle against fixed rent.

And on channel, mobile order-ahead and delivery, plus packaged beans retail, which carry different margin structures.

I'd caveat that I don't know their traffic trend or ticket size — the 15% revenue growth could be all new stores. If per-store traffic is actually declining, that reorders this. Do we have same-store sales?
