[interviewer · RECOMMENDATION · 1:21] Understood. You said a first step of 8 to 10 percent, then measure elasticity. What's the biggest risk to this recommendation, and how would you mitigate it?

[candidate · 1:29] The biggest risk is elasticity — that an 8-10% price increase drives enough traffic loss that revenue falls instead of rising. At 6% margin they have almost no cushion to absorb a volume mistake.

Quick sense of the breakeven: at current economics, COGS is 58% of revenue, so contribution per cup is roughly 42% before labor. If I take 10% price with costs per cup unchanged, I can afford to lose volume until the incremental profit goes flat — very roughly in the high-teens percent of transactions. So they have some room, but not unlimited.

Mitigation: don't do it chain-wide. Pilot in 20-30 stores across different market types — urban, suburban, high versus low competitive density — hold the rest as control, and read transactions and ticket weekly for 6-8 weeks. Lead with the least elastic items, specialty espresso drinks where brand matters most, and hold entry-price drip coffee to protect traffic perception. Pair it with the loyalty program so regulars feel they're getting something back rather than just paying more.
